WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:08.980
This is such a vast
spider web of contacts.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.880
This data that will be transferring

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:15.600
between phones will now be encrypted.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.860
What more do we know about how

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:19.200
this is actually going to work?

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.720
The clamor to improve contact tracing

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:24.160
has seen nations like Israel, Singapore,

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:27.480
South Korea, and of course,
China using a combination

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:29.800
of location data, video camera footage,

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:31.760
and credit card information to track

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:34.580
and contain COVID-19 in their countries.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:37.360
We're prepared, and we're
doing a great job with it.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.920
It will go away, just stay
calm, it will go away.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:41.740
The U.S. has lagged behind,

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:44.300
but Google and Apple
are feverishly building

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:45.700
a contact tracing platform,

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:47.540
scheduled for release in mid-May.

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:50.760
It will enable the use
of Bluetooth technology

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:52.320
to help governments and health agencies

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.020
reduce the spread of the
virus by tracing people

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:57.520
who have come into close
contact with COVID-19.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.740
But how exactly this information
will be used in the future

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:03.480
and with whom is an open question.

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:05.660
Well, we're kind of at this
fascinating moment, right,

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:08.340
where we don't know
exactly what the response

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:09.940
is gonna look like.

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:12.080
We know that robust contact tracing

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:14.600
is gonna be a huge part
of re-opening the economy,

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:17.080
and we know that we have to balance that

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:20.560
with privacy concerns,
somehow, but we don't know

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:22.380
exactly what the balance
is gonna look like.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:24.100
And I leave it up to the
epidemiologists to say

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:28.780
whether you need to be
as repressive as China

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:31.480
or if there are ways to protect privacy.

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:34.960
We might not have a ton
of time to debate it.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:36.760
And that's kind of the problem.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:39.120
There are currently few legal protections

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:42.280
from data misuse or abuse,
and while some states

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:43.880
do have some laws in place,

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:46.160
progress on this front has been halted.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:50.020
There was some privacy
momentum in Congress

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:52.260
before the pandemic hit,

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:54.900
and that's been destroyed
for obvious reasons.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:58.020
California passed a privacy
law a couple of years ago,

00:01:58.060 --> 00:01:59.340
and there's been a lot of work

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:02.860
to try to improve it as it
goes into effect this year.

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:04.920
A bunch of other states
have been considering

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:06.860
privacy legislation as well,

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.320
like Washington State and New York,

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:13.400
and then covid comes and a
lot of the states legislatures

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.300
have just pretty much shut down.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:20.860
Now before you envision
a Black Mirror episode

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:22.920
for your personal future, you should know

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.560
that as of now, involvement
will be voluntary.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:28.000
The data it collects is to be anonymized,

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:29.480
and there will be no central server

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:32.160
where the data is stored, so
governments or corporations

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:34.360
can't directly grab all this private data

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:37.560
for its own purposes, but
that could easily change.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:39.280
There's nothing really to stop them

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:41.160
from having a centralized database.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.840
It's only the restraint
of our expectations.

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:47.440
If our expectations were
to become overturned

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:50.680
and again shift
tremendously over to safety,

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:53.240
it could become a lot more
Orwellian a lot quicker.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.400
There's many scenarios

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:56.800
that could be quite troubling.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:58.000
For example...

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:01.400
Your information could be
given to insurance companies,

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:03.840
and it could be used as
a pre-existing condition

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:05.920
that prevents you from getting insurance

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:10.080
or as a reason to charge
you much higher prices.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:13.480
The information could
be sold to data brokers,

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:17.560
who could then package it
to anyone that they want.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:20.240
The information could end
up with your employer,

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:24.080
who could use it to make decisions about

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:27.560
whether to promote you
because of your health.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:28.800
Now, that may sound a little paranoid,

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:31.920
but we have good reason to be suspicious.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:33.680
These are entities that already collect

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:37.840
enormous amounts of our data
in a commercial context,

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:40.000
and whose mishandling of data

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:41.800
has come under fire in the past.

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:42.720
Exhibit A.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:43.640
Cambridge Analytica.

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:45.020
Cambridge Analytica.

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:46.480
Cambridge Analytica.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:49.900
We just traded away everything there is

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.480
to know about ourselves for Farmville,

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:53.980
and I think that people
are starting to realize

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.940
that wasn't a good trade.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:00.620
In 2016, the big data
company Cambridge Analytica

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:02.700
took the personal data of millions

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:04.240
of Americans from Facebook.

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:06.940
They packaged it and sold
it so that political groups

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:08.860
could then target parts of the population

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:10.620
with narrow casted messages.

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:12.740
People were sharing
information with friends,

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:16.060
and they weren't necessarily
expecting that data

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:19.480
to be shared by a company
like Cambridge Analytica

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:23.360
that was making use of it
to build political profiles,

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:27.740
and then serve specific
advertisements or propaganda.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:29.600
Cambridge Analytica really captures

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:34.420
the dilemma of privacy,
which is how data collected

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:37.020
for one reason, with certain expectations,

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:39.040
is used for another reason.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:41.520
We have been giving away all of our

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:43.380
most private information to companies

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:45.840
that have then sold it on and created

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:50.060
what is now a multi-trillion
dollar a year industry.

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:51.880
This was business as usual

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:54.300
as far as Facebook developers went.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:55.600
This scandal resulted

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:57.080
in the Federal Trade Commission's fining

00:04:57.080 --> 00:04:59.260
of Facebook for five billion dollars,

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:02.620
as well as a general push
for broader privacy laws.

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:06.120
We didn't take a broad enough
view of our responsibility,

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:08.140
and that was a big mistake,

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:11.940
and it was my mistake, and I'm sorry.

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:15.380
What you're seeing is a
greater bipartisan concern

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:18.600
about the power of these data-opolies

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:21.500
and how do we rein them in.

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:22.260
So how do we know this

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:23.800
isn't gonna happen this time?

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:26.360
The answer comes back to
those privacy protection laws

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:29.140
that have been put on hold
because of the pandemic.

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:31.460
If there had been a
national privacy law enacted

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:34.740
before the pandemic hit us,
we probably would've been

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:39.020
in a better position to
respond to it with data.

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:41.720
But even if there was a national

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:44.620
privacy law in place, contact
tracing is actually pointless

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:47.180
if we don't have widespread testing

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.580
to go along with it, which we don't.

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:50.940
So what is this about?

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:53.320
Is this a smokescreen for
collecting a lot of data?

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:58.280
Is the data truly gonna stay
anonymized or aggregated?

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:02.240
The pandemic is showing
why a general privacy law

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:06.320
is so important, because
there's so much confusion

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:08.020
around data collection,

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:10.600
concern that there will be overreach,

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:13.140
and lack of rules about
how the information

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:15.600
could be reused for other purposes.

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:18.020
Laws are going to be needed

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:20.920
to stem a lot of this
corporate data collection,

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:24.760
and I think you're only going
to see those efforts continue.

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:28.000
Short-term, I think they may
be delayed because of covid,

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:31.180
but I think the
fundamental concerns around

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:35.540
overly broad corporate
collection aren't going away.

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:38.320
I don't see some centralized bureaucracy

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:42.240
that will then be shared
with the police and the IRS

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:43.480
and your future employer

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:47.080
and your future insurer,
at least not right now.

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:48.620
I think it is worth noting that

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:50.720
some of those things aren't forbidden.

